# Force LF line endings for cross-platform shell scripts.
#
# Without this file, git's `core.autocrlf` setting on each contributor's
# machine controls how line endings are committed. This repo is developed
# on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, so historically these scripts
# landed in commits with CRLF — which bash on macOS / Linux interprets
# as `\r: command not found` and similar parse failures on the first
# line of the script.
#
# The `text eol=lf` directive forces LF in the working tree on EVERY
# platform regardless of the user's git config, AND normalises any
# pre-existing CRLF when the file is touched.
#
# Re-normalise existing content after editing this file:
#   git add --renormalize .

# Shell scripts must be LF — bash cannot tolerate CRLF on a shebang line.
*.sh              text eol=lf
*.bash            text eol=lf

# Git hooks are POSIX shell — same requirement.
scripts/hooks/*   text eol=lf

# Python venv scripts and setup helpers — these run via `python` directly
# but mixed-encoding venvs cause cryptic activation errors on POSIX shells.
*.py              text eol=lf

# Other text formats — let git infer LF on POSIX-canonical formats.
# These are conventional defaults; the load-bearing line is the *.sh
# rule above.
*.json            text
*.md              text
*.ts              text
*.tsx             text
*.mjs             text
*.cjs             text
*.css             text
*.yml             text
*.yaml            text
*.toml            text

# Binary formats — never normalise.
*.png             binary
*.jpg             binary
*.jpeg            binary
*.gif             binary
*.ico             binary
*.svg             text
*.pdf             binary
*.zip             binary
*.tar             binary
*.gz              binary
*.woff            binary
*.woff2           binary
